confirmed.description:
Three rules flag and rewrite whole comma items without ever consulting `ctx.quoted` or `ctx.rendered`:
- `_repeated`, on every checkpoint;
- `_lowercase`, on Anima;
- `_underscores`, on Kohaku and Anima.

Other rules in the same file do skip quoted text (`_negation` at :711, `_quality_boilerplate` at :1436), and design-notes 2363 says 'the rules skip quoted text'. `comma_items` splits on every comma, including those inside quotes. So the one-click suggestion changes the lettering the user asked to render: it drops repeated pieces, lowercases it, or replaces underscores. The re-check afterwards is clean. The existing tests use tag lists without quotes. The two sources were rated medium and low; the higher is kept because this is a wrong one-click edit on ordinary lettering prompts.

QUOTED's single-quote branch requires that no word character sits before the opening mark or after the closing mark. Python's Unicode word class includes ideographs, so 写着'营业中' and '营业中'的字样 are not quotations. Chinese writes quotes without a space, and ERNIE's own enhancer instruction (excerpt ernie.text) asks for lettering wrapped in English single quotes.

The CJK trigger path also skips a target that starts with a quote mark, so `rendered_spans` is empty. As a result:
- text_to_render is nulled;
- a rewrite that changes the lettering is kept, because its own quotes are not seen by QUOTED either;
- layer-1 lint does not catch it.

QUOTED is the one shared reading of quoted text, so the rules, the encoder's weighting scan and `is_reorder` all miss it too.

`locate()` joins the escaped words with a whitespace pattern, adds no word boundaries, and returns the first hit. That one function decides four things:
- where an issue's quote is (the page selects that span, and `_restated` compares it with finding spans);
- whether a named part is the prompt's own words (`_part`);
- whether quoted rewrite words are lettering (`_lettered`'s fallback, which defeats its own comment "pen is not in OPEN");
- whether a quote in a rewrite is invented lettering (`_check_rewrite`).

A short word that also occurs inside an earlier, longer word is found there. The tests never exercise this: FakeCritic always quotes the prompt's first word, and test_quotes_are_located_in_the_prompt_or_nulled has no mid-word case.

`build_context` turns each Excerpt into `Citation(text, url, 'author')`. Neither `Citation` nor `CritiqueIssue` has a note field, so an excerpt's note reaches only the critic's system message. `Excerpt.original` is never read anywhere in backend/.

The page shows 'A<n> · Authors', with the excerpt text as the tooltip and no note. By contrast, the Order section does show the same excerpts' notes, via author_orders. Affected excerpts:
- ERNIE, Z-Image and Qwen: the app's translations of the authors' words;
- NetaYume: excerpts inherited from Neta Lumina's prompt book;
- klein: BFL's FLUX.2 [pro]/[max] guide;
- Krea [dev]: BFL's family guide.

This contradicts CLAUDE.md ('a borrowed excerpt says whose it is'), design-notes 2311-2313 and the SOURCE_LABELS comment.

`build_context` reads only `applied.negative_prompt`. That one value decides three things:
- whether the user message says 'Negative prompt (it runs)';
- whether the rewrite rule adds 'No negative prompt runs';
- the input_tokens that `check_fits` counts.

Every critic test passes the same GenerateRequest as both request and applied, and the route test checks only the arguments FakeCritic recorded. A mutant that reads `request.negative_prompt` instead survives the whole suite. That leaves CLAUDE.md's 'the negative prompt is measured and checked only as applied() runs it' unguarded for the critic, on the default checkpoint where applied() routinely drops the negative.

Every x86-64 Linux is mapped to ubuntu-cuda-13.4-x64, so `supported()` is True. That build needs:
- libgomp.so.1, which is not in the tree (RUNPATH is $ORIGIN only);
- libssl.so.3 and libcrypto.so.3;
- GLIBC_2.38 and GLIBCXX_3.4.32.

A fresh Ubuntu WSL image lacks libgomp1: here it arrived only via g++, and setup.sh treats having no compiler as fine. Ubuntu 22.04 and Debian 12 cannot run the build at all. verified.md's ldd row was measured after libgomp1 was already installed. The error is accurate and the downloads stay cached, so the severity is low.

CLAUDE.md: 'Check works cold: CPU tokenizers and rules off the loop, never ensure_loaded, never the gate'. The cold test asserts only that no load happened, and no test posts Check while a job runs or the gate is held. Each of these mutants passes the full suite:
- routing Check through run_when_idle (a 500 BackendBusy while any job runs);
- refusing Check when busy;
- calling `_analyse` inline on the event loop, in the analyze route or in the critique's analysis.

The critique's rewrite lint is guarded only by a gate check.
